# 132 · Rig images for each GPU generation: CUDA 12 for Pascal and Volta, CUDA 13 on amd64 and arm64 for Blackwell and DGX Spark

**Status:** `IN_PROGRESS` · **Version:** [v0.1.3](../roadmap.md#v013) · **Depends on:** [010](010-published-images.goal)

## What

Thermite publishes one CUDA harness image today: CUDA 12.6.3 on Ubuntu 22.04, for amd64 (goal 010). CUDA 12 still builds for Pascal (P100, compute capability 6.0) and Volta (V100, 7.0), so that image serves both; CUDA 12.6 does not know the newest GPUs, and it has no arm64 build. CUDA 13 removed offline compilation and library support for Maxwell, Pascal and Volta ([CUDA features archive](https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-features-archive/index.html)), and NVIDIA's DGX Spark has an Arm CPU and a Blackwell GPU, the GB10, of compute capability 12.1 ([NVIDIA's forum](https://forums.developer.nvidia.com/t/dgx-spark-gb10-cuda-compute-capability/342864)).

So rigs need two CUDA harness images, both published and checked by the release:

- **CUDA 12** (today's `-cuda` image), amd64: Pascal, Volta, Turing, Ampere and Hopper, P100 and V100 included.
- **CUDA 13**, amd64 and arm64: Turing and newer, Blackwell included, and the DGX Spark on arm64.

Control sets up each GPU rig from the image that fits its GPUs: by the GPU's compute capability, which control reads when it checks a machine or a cluster's nodes, or as the owner chooses on Add a rig. The GPU smoke kit builds for the architectures of the toolkit it runs with (today: Pascal to Hopper, with PTX), so the rig check passes on both images.

## Why

The owner, 2026-10-24: "we need a P100 image, a V100 image, and a DGX Spark image for rigs. if P100/V100 can be the same great. But DGX Spark needs CUDA 13 right?"

## Done when

- [ ] The release builds, publishes and checks a CUDA 13 harness image for amd64 and arm64, beside the CUDA 12 one, with tags that say which is which. (Built: verified at the first publish.)
- [ ] Control picks a GPU rig's CUDA image by its GPUs' compute capability, and Add a rig shows which image it will use and lets the owner choose the other.
- [ ] The GPU smoke kit builds for each toolkit's architectures, and the rig check passes with the CUDA 12 image on a P100 or V100 rig and with the CUDA 13 image on a DGX Spark.
- [ ] The release bundle and control's settings name both images; the pages for the images, the settings, Add a rig and the compatibility list say which GPU takes which.

## Open questions

- Whether the RunPod rig image needs a CUDA 13 variant too, for Blackwell pods, or the CUDA 12 image's PTX is enough there.

## Decisions

- 2026-10-25, owner: v0.1.0's scope is frozen, and this goal moves to v0.1.2. Its CUDA 13 image has never run on a GPU. The owner chose to publish it with v0.1.0, labelled untested on the release's README, the harness image, compatibility and known-limits pages and the changelog, rather than take it out of the release tooling before the tag.

- 2026-10-24, owner: P100 and V100 may share one image; DGX Spark needs CUDA 13.
- 2026-10-25, in the build: the CUDA 13 base is `nvidia/cuda:13.0.3-devel-ubuntu24.04` (amd64 and arm64), pinned by digest. 13.0 is the CUDA of NVIDIA's 580 drivers, which the lab's GPU hosts run, so the image needs no newer driver; later 13.x toolkits would. The tags: `-cuda` stays CUDA 12 (labs pin it), and `-cuda13` is new. The RunPod rig image stays on CUDA 12.

## Progress

- 2026-10-24: Goal written from the owner's words, with CUDA 13's dropped architectures and the GB10's compute capability checked against NVIDIA's pages.
- 2026-10-25: part 1 (branch `feat/132-cuda13-image`): the release builds `harness-cuda13` from the same Containerfile on the CUDA 13 base, for amd64 and arm64 (`cuda_base` names each CUDA entry's base; only the CUDA 12 entry builds the rig image, `rig`), tags it `-cuda13` with the version, and lists it in the bundle's readme. The checks read each image's platforms from its index and hold it to those in `src/release/bundle.ts` (`it is not published for linux/arm64`). The smoke kit builds for its toolkit's architectures: CUDA 12, `sm_60` to `sm_90`; CUDA 13, `sm_75` to `sm_120` (its code runs on the GB10's `sm_121`), each with PTX. Placeholders may hold digits now (`@@HARNESS_CUDA13_IMAGE@@`). Pages: Harness image, Compatibility, Rig checks, Settings, Push the images to your own registry. Left: control picks the image by compute capability (part 2, with a mockup of Add a rig first), and the checks on real GPUs.

